{% from 'ingressroute.yml.j2' import monitoring_route with context %}
---
{#
  Three reads, one prefix: monitor-bridge's check_prowlarr_indexers reads /api/v1/indexerstatus
  and /api/v1/indexer, and postflight.py's §9.3 API-key check reads /api/v1/indexer because it
  is the one this route admits. The prefix covers those and covers nothing else —
  /api/v1/indexer is not a prefix of any write endpoint the checks do not use.

  extra_client_cidrs admits the two cluster nodes' own host processes, which is what postflight
  is. Without it §9.3 was structurally SKIP whenever prowlarr's pod sat on daniel-server —
  which it did when #1642 was measured: the ClusterIP answers only a caller on the pod's own
  node, and this route admitted `k8s_bridge_client_ip` alone, so the request fell through to
  prowlarr's own Authelia'd route and 302'd.

  These are cni0 GATEWAY addresses, not the nodes' LAN addresses: host traffic to the ingress
  VIP SNATs to them. Read off Traefik's own access log on 2026-09-10 — a request postflight
  made from daniel-box arrived as `ClientHost: 10.42.0.1`, so a `k8s_node_client_ip` (10.0.0.215)
  grant matched nothing and the SKIP survived its own fix. Same pair and the same reasoning as
  loki-homelab's read route, whose `DECIDED 2026-08-24` marker verified it live first. A pod
  arrives with its own 10.42.x.y address rather than a gateway, so this is the two nodes and
  nothing else.

  The path prefix, the LAN-only rule and prowlarr's own X-Api-Key are unchanged.
#}
{{ monitoring_route(container_item.name, container_item.hostname, container_item.port, '/api/v1/indexer',
                    extra_client_cidrs=k3s_cni0_gateways) }}
